# GodMode — local configuration sample
#
# Copy to `.env` and edit. `.env` is gitignored; this file is the committed reference.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing here is a secret. There is no backend, no API key, and no account — the only
# settings that exist are about where the app listens.


# ─── Port ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# READ THIS BEFORE CHANGING IT.
#
# Your entire history lives in the browser's IndexedDB, and a browser keys that storage by
# ORIGIN — scheme, host and port together. So `localhost:5173` and `localhost:5174` are two
# separate, unrelated databases.
#
# Changing this value therefore does not move your data. It points the app at a different,
# empty database, and the app will open on the Welcome screen as though you had never used
# it. Your old history is still there, intact, at the old port.
#
# If you do need to change it: Settings -> Export backup first, change the port, then
# Settings -> Restore from a backup.
#
# `dev` and `preview` deliberately use this same port, so switching between them keeps you on
# one origin. That means they cannot run at the same time — which is the intended trade.
#
# Startup fails loudly if the port is already taken (strictPort), rather than quietly moving
# to the next free one and stranding your history.
#
GODMODE_PORT=5173


# ─── Host ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Leave unset (the default) to listen on localhost only.
#
# Set to 0.0.0.0 to reach the app from a phone on the same network — find your machine's LAN
# address and open http://192.168.x.x:5173 on the phone.
#
# Worth knowing what that does and does not give you:
#
#   - It works. You can run a session from the phone while on your own WiFi.
#   - It is plain HTTP, so iOS will NOT register a service worker. No "Add to Home Screen"
#     app, no offline. Lose WiFi mid-workout and the page is gone.
#   - The phone is a different origin again, so it gets its own database. Desktop history does
#     not appear there. Move it with Export backup / Restore.
#
# Home-screen install and offline use need HTTPS, which means hosting the built `dist/`
# somewhere with a certificate. See the README.
#
# GODMODE_HOST=0.0.0.0


# ─── Server / accounts (self-hosting) ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The sections above configure the Vite DEV server (local-first, IndexedDB, no accounts).
# When you run the real SERVER (`npm run serve`, or a deployment), it has per-user accounts
# and SQLite storage, configured by the variables below. See `server/DEPLOY.md` for the full
# table; these are the ones you touch most.

# Registration gate. `invite` (default) requires GODMODE_INVITE_CODE; `open` lets anyone
# register; `closed` accepts no new accounts. Flipping to open later is just this one value.
# GODMODE_REGISTRATION=invite
# GODMODE_INVITE_CODE=change-me-to-a-shared-code

# Dev-only convenience: when set, `POST /api/session` accepts { token } for the bootstrap owner.
# `npm run serve` sets this for you from the generated token file. Leave UNSET in production —
# real accounts replaced the old always-on shared token (LBV-1480).
# GODMODE_DEV_TOKEN=

# Google Sign-In (optional). With no client id/secret the Google button is hidden and
# /auth/google/* returns 404; password accounts still work. The redirect URI is
# GODMODE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN + /auth/google/callback unless GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI overrides it, and it
# must match the URI registered with Google exactly.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GODMODE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-host.example
# GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=

# AGPL §13 "Source code" footer link (BUILD-TIME client var — must be VITE_-prefixed). Unset,
# it points at the JustClose fork.
# VITE_GODMODE_REPO_URL=https://github.com/lennertbewernick/godmode
